{
 "summary": "Moves block and undo file flushing, block index writing, and pruned file deletion out of validation's FlushStateToDisk and into BlockManager. Validation continues to determine prunable height limits and targets, passing them as explicit structs to BlockManager. BlockManager now handles prune locks, selecting files, tracking pending unlinks across failed flushes, and managing disk space checks.",
 "problem": "FlushStateToDisk directly inspects and manipulates block storage internals (prune locks, pruning flags, blocks directory disk checks, and file deletions), while BlockManager inspects chainstate directly. This tight coupling prevents block storage from offering a clean standalone pruning API for libbitcoinkernel.",
 "evidence": [
  "\"A pruning API on block storage (#35627) would have to go through validation for all of it.\" (PR description)",
  "Linked issue #35627 describes kernel's inability to work with a pruned blocksdir due to storage and validation coupling."
 ],
 "dependencies": {
  "depends_on": [
   "#36735"
  ],
  "enables": [
   "#35627",
   "#36739"
  ]
 },
 "scope_notes": "This PR is stacked on #36735 and contains its commits (including the bugfix and tests for deleting pruned files on later flushes), before applying the BlockManager refactoring commits.",
 "changed_since_previous": "Now provides the full patch and commit history; the structural approach moving block writes and pruning into BlockManager remains identical to the previous description.",
 "card": "This PR refactors block storage writing, index updates, and pruning bookkeeping out of FlushStateToDisk into BlockManager. Validation calculates prunable height ranges and targets and passes them down, leaving prune locks, file selection, index updates, and unlinking to storage. This decoupling addresses architecture needs tracked in issue #35627 for libbitcoinkernel. It depends on and includes commits from PR #36735.",
